<p>Iran’s Assembly of Experts has selected Mojtaba Khamenei, the son of the late Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, as Iran’s new supreme leader, according to Iranian state media and multiple international outlets. The move follows the killing of Ali Khamenei in strikes that several reports describe as part of a joint U.S.-Israeli campaign against Iran. Fighting continued as the appointment was announced. Several outlets describe Mojtaba Khamenei as a low-profile, mid-ranking cleric who built influence behind the scenes through close ties to Iran’s security apparatus—especially the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC). After the selection, the IRGC pledged allegiance to him. Commentators have framed the choice as a bid for continuity and a way for Iran’s power centers to project stability during wartime, even as questions persist inside and outside Iran about hereditary-style succession—and what it could mean for any future opening to the West. The leadership change also comes with broader regional and global spillovers. In some reporting, oil prices moved above $100 a barrel, and markets reacted to fears of energy-supply disruption as strikes widened to infrastructure targets.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/HbSJAXFk_bkCKVNeVtWl_y-shdcWAmwlB8JBswDbFgjqi7mylMyw6iQstjpxC643f8HOALIsBPjEriHTDKzpHMffbGsLiwdHOKjft8jEuyms' alt='Mojtaba Khamenei, pictured in Tehran in October 2024, in a Reuters file photo.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Background and networks: Coverage says Mojtaba Khamenei volunteered in the Iran-Iraq war and built relationships with Iran’s military services—ties that later strengthened his standing with the security establishment.</li><li>Targeting rhetoric: Euronews reported that Israel has earmarked Mojtaba Khamenei as a potential target after his elevation.</li><li>Sanctions history: The U.S. Treasury sanctioned Mojtaba Khamenei in 2019, saying he represented the supreme leader in an official capacity despite holding no elected or formal government post.</li><li>Widening infrastructure risk: Some reports say the war has spread beyond military sites to fuel depots, energy routes, and desalination facilities—raising the risk of disruptions to essential infrastructure across the region.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian state media/Assembly of Experts: State media said the Assembly of Experts moved quickly to select Mojtaba Khamenei and urged Iranians to rally behind him for national unity during the crisis. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/mojtaba-khamenei-iran-new-supreme-leader/article70720317.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC): The IRGC publicly pledged allegiance to Mojtaba Khamenei after the selection, underscoring its role as a key institutional backer. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/3/9/irans-irgc-backs-mojtaba-khamenei-as-new-supreme-leader'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Assembly member Ayatollah Mohsen Heidari Alekasir: A council member said the chosen successor matched guidance that Iran’s top leader should be “hated by the enemy,” as cited in regional reporting. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1979991/khameneis-son-mojtaba-named-new-supreme-leader'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iranian government opponents/critics: Opponents warned that Mojtaba Khamenei could rule “with an iron fist like his father,” The New York Times reported. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/08/world/middleeast/iran-mojtaba-khamenei-supreme-leader-reactions.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Pope Leo: Pope Leo warned of Middle East “hatred and fear,” as cited in Reuters-distributed coverage published by Al-Monitor. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/khameneis-hardline-son-mojtaba-appointed-irans-new-leader-pope-leo-warns-middle'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/mojtaba-khamenei-iran-new-supreme-leader/article70720317.ece'>Iran names Khamenei&#x27;s 